  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy uses a dynamic programming approach to construct h as a piecewise constant function with minimal maximum overlap. It exploits the problem's structure by treating h as a collection of intervals with controlled jumps, allowing systematic exploration of configurations that minimize overlap without local minima issues. The approach balances global exploration and local refinement through interval splitting and merging, guided by explicit overlap calculations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: h represented by intervals [(a\u2081, b\u2081, v\u2081), ..., (a\u2c7c, b\u2c7c, v\u2c7c)] where a\u2081=0, b\u2c7c=2, \u03a3(v\u2c7c*(b\u2c7c-a\u2c7c))=1, 0 \u2264 v\u2c7c \u22641  \nObjective: Minimize max\u2096 {\u03a3 [v_i * (1 - v_j) * max(0, min(b_j, a_i +k) - max(a_j, a_i +k))] for all i,j}  \nConstraints: Interval partition of [0,2], integral=1\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize with coarse intervals based on initial_h_values or uniform split\nb. Dynamic programming loop:\n   i. For each interval, consider split points and new value assignments\n   ii. Compute overlap for candidate configurations\n   iii. Select configuration with minimal maximum overlap\nc. Refinement criteria: Interval length, value differences, overlap impact\nd. Merge adjacent intervals with identical values\ne. Track feasible solutions and ensure integral constraint\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial interval count: 3 or 4\n- Splitting strategy: Midpoint or adaptive based on overlap\n- Value adjustment: Linear interpolation or binary search\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Budget allocation: 70% for DP, 30% for overlap computations\n- Robustness features: Interval merging, feasibility checks\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Check interval constraints, sum integral=1\n- Validate overlap computation matches evaluator",
